INSERT INTO `3rdtermengexamquest926` VALUES (1,'&lt;p&gt;Read the following passage below and answer the questions that follow.&lt;br /&gt;\nAll of us are familiar with money. All of us have bought one thing or the other with&lt;br /&gt;\nmoney. Day in day out, we still continue to depend on money. Everyone continues to&lt;br /&gt;\nlook for money. Indeed, nobody can exist in the society without money as it is used to&lt;br /&gt;\npurchase man&amp;rsquo;s basic needs.&lt;br /&gt;\nIn modern times, the money we spend takes the form of a piece of paper (note) or metal&lt;br /&gt;\n(coin). Money however has not been in the form of paper or metal as we know it today.&lt;br /&gt;\nBefore the advent of money, people exchanged goods through the system of trade by&lt;br /&gt;\nbatter. In this system, goods that one produced which another person wanted were&lt;br /&gt;\nexchanged for goods that other person produced and which one wanted. This meant&lt;br /&gt;\nthat if you produced a commodity and you needed another commodity you had to look&lt;br /&gt;\nfor somebody who had the commodity you needed and who also needed the commodity&lt;br /&gt;\nyou had. For an example, if I had yam and I wanted meat, I had to look for somebody&lt;br /&gt;\nwho had meat and wanted yam so that we could exchange. Obviously, it was a highly&lt;br /&gt;\ninconvenient and tedious way of getting what one needed.&lt;br /&gt;\nLater on in Nigerian history cowries were used as money. A cowry is the white shell of a&lt;br /&gt;\nsmall sea creature. To make counting easy, cowries were strung together in twenties.&lt;br /&gt;\nEach bundle of twenty cowries was referred to as head. People who had large sums of&lt;br /&gt;\nmoney had taken them in bags with each bag containing 20,000 cowries. This system of&lt;br /&gt;\nusing cowry as a medium of exchange brought with it the problem of carrying cowries&lt;br /&gt;\nabout. Rich people therefore had to employ able-bodied men to carry money for them.&lt;br /&gt;\n1. Why is money very important in the society?&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;used to purchase man&amp;rsquo;s basic&lt;br /&gt;\nneeds&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;use for ceremonies&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;use to buy anything&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;use to correct wrong.&lt;/p&gt;\n','','A',0.5,'',0),(2,'&lt;p&gt;Read the following passage below and answer the questions that follow.&lt;br /&gt;\nAll of us are familiar with money. All of us have bought one thing or the other with&lt;br /&gt;\nmoney. Day in day out, we still continue to depend on money. Everyone continues to&lt;br /&gt;\nlook for money. Indeed, nobody can exist in the society without money as it is used to&lt;br /&gt;\npurchase man&amp;rsquo;s basic needs.&lt;br /&gt;\nIn modern times, the money we spend takes the form of a piece of paper (note) or metal&lt;br /&gt;\n(coin). Money however has not been in the form of paper or metal as we know it today.&lt;br /&gt;\nBefore the advent of money, people exchanged goods through the system of trade by&lt;br /&gt;\nbatter. In this system, goods that one produced which another person wanted were&lt;br /&gt;\nexchanged for goods that other person produced and which one wanted. This meant&lt;br /&gt;\nthat if you produced a commodity and you needed another commodity you had to look&lt;br /&gt;\nfor somebody who had the commodity you needed and who also needed the commodity&lt;br /&gt;\nyou had. For an example, if I had yam and I wanted meat, I had to look for somebody&lt;br /&gt;\nwho had meat and wanted yam so that we could exchange. Obviously, it was a highly&lt;br /&gt;\ninconvenient and tedious way of getting what one needed.&lt;br /&gt;\nLater on in Nigerian history cowries were used as money. A cowry is the white shell of a&lt;br /&gt;\nsmall sea creature. To make counting easy, cowries were strung together in twenties.&lt;br /&gt;\nEach bundle of twenty cowries was referred to as head. People who had large sums of&lt;br /&gt;\nmoney had taken them in bags with each bag containing 20,000 cowries. This system of&lt;br /&gt;\nusing cowry as a medium of exchange brought with it the problem of carrying cowries&lt;br /&gt;\nabout. Rich people therefore had to employ able-bodied men to carry money for them.&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;br /&gt;\n2. Before money came into existence, how did people get the goods they wanted?&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\nBy selling&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;trade by batter&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;by buying&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;by non of the above&lt;/p&gt;\n','','B',0.5,'',0),(3,'&lt;p&gt;Read the following passage below and answer the questions that follow.&lt;br /&gt;\nAll of us are familiar with money. All of us have bought one thing or the other with&lt;br /&gt;\nmoney. Day in day out, we still continue to depend on money. Everyone continues to&lt;br /&gt;\nlook for money. Indeed, nobody can exist in the society without money as it is used to&lt;br /&gt;\npurchase man&amp;rsquo;s basic needs.&lt;br /&gt;\nIn modern times, the money we spend takes the form of a piece of paper (note) or metal&lt;br /&gt;\n(coin). Money however has not been in the form of paper or metal as we know it today.&lt;br /&gt;\nBefore the advent of money, people exchanged goods through the system of trade by&lt;br /&gt;\nbatter. In this system, goods that one produced which another person wanted were&lt;br /&gt;\nexchanged for goods that other person produced and which one wanted. This meant&lt;br /&gt;\nthat if you produced a commodity and you needed another commodity you had to look&lt;br /&gt;\nfor somebody who had the commodity you needed and who also needed the commodity&lt;br /&gt;\nyou had. For an example, if I had yam and I wanted meat, I had to look for somebody&lt;br /&gt;\nwho had meat and wanted yam so that we could exchange. Obviously, it was a highly&lt;br /&gt;\ninconvenient and tedious way of getting what one needed.&lt;br /&gt;\nLater on in Nigerian history cowries were used as money. A cowry is the