and I wanted meat, I had to look for somebody&lt;br /&gt;\nwho had meat and wanted yam so that we could exchange. Obviously, it was a highly&lt;br /&gt;\ninconvenient and tedious way of getting what one needed.&lt;br /&gt;\nLater on in Nigerian history cowries were used as money. A cowry is the white shell of a&lt;br /&gt;\nsmall sea creature. To make counting easy, cowries were strung together in twenties.&lt;br /&gt;\nEach bundle of twenty cowries was referred to as head. People who had large sums of&lt;br /&gt;\nmoney had taken them in bags with each bag containing 20,000 cowries. This system of&lt;br /&gt;\nusing cowry as a medium of exchange brought with it the problem of carrying cowries&lt;br /&gt;\nabout. Rich people therefore had to employ able-bodied men to carry money for them.&lt;br /&gt;\n4. What is a cowry?&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;mood money&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;Silver money&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;white shell of a small sea&lt;br /&gt;\ncreature&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;paper money&lt;/p&gt;\n','','C',0.5,'',0),(5,'&lt;p&gt;Read the following passage below and answer the questions that follow.&lt;br /&gt;\nAll of us are familiar with money. All of us have bought one thing or the other with&lt;br /&gt;\nmoney. Day in day out, we still continue to depend on money. Everyone continues to&lt;br /&gt;\nlook for money. Indeed, nobody can exist in the society without money as it is used to&lt;br /&gt;\npurchase man&amp;rsquo;s basic needs.&lt;br /&gt;\nIn modern times, the money we spend takes the form of a piece of paper (note) or metal&lt;br /&gt;\n(coin). Money however has not been in the form of paper or metal as we know it today.&lt;br /&gt;\nBefore the advent of money, people exchanged goods through the system of trade by&lt;br /&gt;\nbatter. In this system, goods that one produced which another person wanted were&lt;br /&gt;\nexchanged for goods that other person produced and which one wanted. This meant&lt;br /&gt;\nthat if you produced a commodity and you needed another commodity you had to look&lt;br /&gt;\nfor somebody who had the commodity you needed and who also needed the commodity&lt;br /&gt;\nyou had. For an example, if I had yam and I wanted meat, I had to look for somebody&lt;br /&gt;\nwho had meat and wanted yam so that we could exchange. Obviously, it was a highly&lt;br /&gt;\ninconvenient and tedious way of getting what one needed.&lt;br /&gt;\nLater on in Nigerian history cowries were used as money. A cowry is the white shell of a&lt;br /&gt;\nsmall sea creature. To make counting easy, cowries were strung together in twenties.&lt;br /&gt;\nEach bundle of twenty cowries was referred to as head. People who had large sums of&lt;br /&gt;\nmoney had taken them in bags with each bag containing 20,000 cowries. This system of&lt;br /&gt;\nusing cowry as a medium of exchange brought with it the problem of carrying cowries&lt;br /&gt;\nabout. Rich people therefore had to employ able-bodied men to carry money for them.&lt;br /&gt;\n5. Why were cowries counted in twenties?&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;Because they are much&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;make&lt;br /&gt;\ncounting it easy&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;desire purpose&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;detail counting&lt;/p&gt;\n','','B',0.5,'',0),(6,'&lt;p&gt;All members of Basic 7 students love _______&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;each other&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;one another&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\nthemselves&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;herself&lt;/p&gt;\n','','B',0.5,'',0),(7,'&lt;p&gt;The function of the THE CHILDRENin this sentence &amp;lsquo;The children are playing in the&lt;br /&gt;\ngarden&amp;rsquo; is ______.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;compliment&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;subject&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;plural&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;object&lt;/p&gt;\n','','B',0.5,'',0),(8,'&lt;p&gt;__________conjunctions are used in pairs.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;Coordinating&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;Correlative&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\nSubordinating&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;Common&lt;/p&gt;\n','','B',0.5,'',0),(9,'&lt;p&gt;Jacob is ___________than his sister.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;brilliant&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;more brilliant&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;Much brilliant&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;so brilliant&lt;/p&gt;\n','','B',0.5,'',0),(10,'&lt;p&gt;I can&amp;#39;t see_____ book on the shelf.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;much&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;none&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;some&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;any&lt;/p&gt;\n','','D',0.5,'',0),(11,'&lt;p&gt;The car did not go far because it ran ___________of petrol.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;away&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;in&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;out&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;off&lt;/p&gt;\n','','C',0.5,'',0),(12,'&lt;p&gt;He writes______ legibly than his brother.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;more&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;most&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;much&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;very&lt;/p&gt;\n','','A',0.5,'',0),(13,'&lt;p&gt;Obi has been transferred to a new school_____ last year.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;at&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;during&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;.since&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;for&lt;/p&gt;\n','','C',0.5,'',0),(14,'&lt;p&gt;The book was_____ difficult to read.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;much&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;too&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;little&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;such&lt;/p&gt;\n','','B',0.5,'',0),(15,'&lt;p&gt;It___________ here in Yola during the football match.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;rain&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;rained&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\nraining&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;rains&lt;/p&gt;\n','','B',0.5,'',0),(16,'&lt;p&gt;The man has the building project, _______?&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;doesn&amp;rsquo;t he&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;did he&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;he didn&amp;#39;t&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;he&lt;br /&gt;\ndid not.&lt;/p&gt;\n','','A',0.5,'',0),(17,'&lt;p&gt;She ____ beautifully every day.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;sings&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;Sang&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;Sung&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;singing&lt;/p&gt;\n','','A',0.5,'',0),(18,'&lt;p&gt;When two pure vo