INSERT INTO `dlhsentranceexams2quest680` VALUES (53,'Find 2/3 of 2 ½ hours in minutes','50','60','100','150','','C',1,'',51),(54,'Find the area of a rectangle with length 6.2cm and breadth 5cm.','16cm2','18cm2','24cm2','31cm2','','D',1,'',52),(55,'In an examination, 35 out of 125 students failed. What percentage of the students passed?','28%','35%','72%','65%','','C',1,'',53),(56,'The following data are recorded by a student in the laboratory: 6, 8, 5, 4, 5, 6, 5, 5, 8, 8. Find the mode.','4','5','6','8','','B',1,'',54),(57,'A boy has ‘k’ kobo. He spends 15 kobo. If he has 32 kobo left, what is the value of k?','6','17','37','47','','D',1,'',55),(58,'Find the median of 6, 2, 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 2, 0, 2, 3, 4, 2, 3, 5.','1','2','3','4','','B',1,'',56),(59,'Simplify 2n x 6 + 8n.','60n','28n','20n','16n','','C',1,'',57),(60,'If x : 6 = 15 : 30, find the value of x.','2','3','6','12','','B',1,'',58),(61,'The place value of the 3 in 24.635 is','3 thousandths','3 hundredths','3 tens','3 tenths','','B',1,'',59),(62,'What number must be added to 343 to make it a perfect square?','13','15','18','20','','C',1,'',60),(63,'&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;INSTRUCTIONS: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Answer all questions in this section.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;em&gt;Read the two passages below carefully and answer the questions under them. Shade the&lt;br /&gt;\nanswer space in the answer sheet which bears the same letter as the answer you have&lt;br /&gt;\nchosen. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PASSAGE ONE &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;Once upon a time, in the village of Jassaca, there lived a rich old man. The man had only one son but many slaves. He thought about what was best to do for the boy and decided to send him to school to acquire knowledge which would help him to spend his wealth sensibly.&lt;br /&gt;\nWhile the young boy was away, the old man became seriously ill and fearing that he might die, he decided to write his will. He was worried about how many of his property was best to leave to his son and what to do with the slaves. Fearing that the slaves might kill his son if he left all his property to him, he decided to leave his entire property to the chief slave except for one small part of it which he would allow his son to choose. The chief slave was happy at this news, since it meant that he would inherit&lt;br /&gt;\nalmost the entire property and the son would have only a part of it.&lt;br /&gt;\nThe old man soon died. When the son heard of his father&amp;rsquo;s death, he felt dejected and when he was told of the contents of the will, he felt worse. The villagers gathered to share out the property in accordance with what was stated in the will. The boy was confused as to which part of the property to choose. His father&amp;rsquo;s closest friend, who had come to console him, advised him. The boy cleared his throat and&lt;br /&gt;\nannounced that what he wanted was the chief slave himself since, after all, he was a part his father&amp;rsquo;s property.&lt;br /&gt;\nIn this way, the son inherited his father&amp;rsquo;s entire property because whatever belonged to the chief slave belonged to the master of the chief slave. The villagers were happy at the young boy&amp;rsquo;s wise choice but the chief slave was extremely upset by it.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:14px&quot;&gt;The old man left his property to the chief slave because&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;he hated his son&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;he loved the chief slave&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;he feared the slaves might kill his son if he did not&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;his son was still at school&lt;/p&gt;\n','the chief slave had nursed him in his illness','C',1,'',61),(64,'&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;INSTRUCTIONS: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Answer all questions in this section.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;em&gt;Read the two passages below carefully and answer the questions under them. Shade the&lt;br /&gt;\nanswer space in the answer sheet which bears the same letter as the answer you have&lt;br /&gt;\nchosen. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PASSAGE ONE &lt;/strong&gt;Once upon a time, in the village of Jassaca, there lived a rich old man. The man had only one son but many slaves. He thought about what was best to do for the boy and decided to send him to school to acquire knowledge which would help him to spend his wealth sensibly.&lt;br /&gt;\nWhile the young boy was away, the old man became seriously ill and fearing that he might die, he decided to write his will. He was worried about how many of his property was best to leave to his son and what to do with the slaves. Fearing that the slaves might kill his son if he left all his property to him, he decided to leave his entire property to the chief slave except for one small part of it which he would allow his son to choose. The chief slave was happy at this news, since it meant that he would inherit&lt;br /&gt;\nalmost the entire property and the son would have only a part of it.&lt;br /&gt;\nThe old man soon died. When the son heard of his father&amp;rsquo;s death, he felt dejected and when he was told of the contents of the will, he felt worse. The villagers gathered to share out the property in accordance with what was stated in the will. The boy was confused as to which part of the property to choose. His father&amp;rsquo;s closest friend, who had come to console him, advised him. The boy cleared his throat and&lt;br /&gt;\nannounced that what he wanted was the chief slave himself since, after all, he was a part his father&amp;rsquo;s property.&lt;br /&gt;\nIn this way, the son inherited his father&amp;rsquo;s entire property because whatever belonged to the chief slave belonged to the master of the chief slave. The villagers were happy at the young boy&amp;rsquo;s wise choice but the chief slave was extremely upset by it.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;The old man became sick&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;while the chief slave was away&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;while his son was away at school&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;when he lived in the village&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;because nobody was around to care for him&lt;/p&gt;\n','because he had been poisoned ','B',1,'',62),(65,'&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;INSTRUCTIONS: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Answer all questions in this section.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;em&gt;Read the two passages below carefully and answer the questions under them. Shade the&lt;br /&gt;\nanswer space in the answer sheet which bears the same letter as the answer you have&lt;br /&gt;\nchosen. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PASSAGE ONE &lt;/strong&gt;Once upon a time, in the village of Jassaca, there lived a rich old man. The man had only one son but many slaves. He thought about what was best to do for the boy and decided to send him to school to acquire knowledge which would help him to spend his wealth sensibly.&lt;br /&gt;\nWhile the young boy was away, the old man became seriously ill and fearing that he might die, he decided to write his will. He was worried about how many of his property was best to leave to his son and what to do with the slaves. Fearing that the slaves might kill his son if he left all his property to him, he decided to leave his entire property to the chief slave except for one small part of it which he would allow his son to choose. The chief slave was happy at this news, since it meant that he would inherit&lt;br /&gt;\nalmost the entire property and the son would have only a part of it.&lt;br /&gt;\nThe old man soon died. When the son heard of his father&amp;rsquo;s death, he felt dejected and when he was told of the contents of the will, he felt worse. The villagers gathered to share out the property in accordance with what was stated in the will. The boy was confused as to which part of the property to choose. His father&amp;rsquo;s closest friend, who had come to console him, advised him. The boy cleared his throat and&lt;br /&gt;\nannounced that what he wanted was the chief slave himself since, after all, he was a part his father&amp;rsquo;s property.&lt;br /&gt;\nIn this way, the son inherited his father&amp;rsquo;s entire property because whatever belonged to the chief slave belonged to the master of the chief slave. The villagers were happy at the young boy&amp;rsquo;s wise choice but the chief slave was extremely upset by it.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;The old man sent his son to school&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;because he wanted him to acquire knowledge&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;so that he could die in his absence&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;because he wanted to live alone with the slave&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;so that he could write his will&lt;/p&gt;\n','because he was ill','C',1,'',63),(66,'&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;INSTRUCTIONS: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Answer all questions in this section.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;em&gt;Read the two passages below carefully and answer the questions under them. Shade the&lt;br /&gt;\nanswer space in the answer sheet which bears the same letter as the answer you have&lt;br /&gt;\nchosen. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PASSAGE ONE &lt;/strong&gt;Once upon a time, in the village of Jassaca, there lived a rich old man. The man had only one son but many slaves. He thought about what was best to do for the boy and decided to send him to school to acquire knowledge which would help him to spend his wealth sensibly.&lt;br /&gt;\nWhile the young boy was away, the old man became seriously ill and fearing that he might die, he decided to write his will. He was worried about how many of his property was best to leave to his son and what to do with the slaves. Fearing that the slaves might kill his son if he left all his property to him, he decided to leave his entire property to the chief slave except for one small part of it which he would allow his son to choose. The chief slave was happy at this news, since it meant that he would inherit almost the entire property and the son would have only a part of it.&lt;br /&gt;\nThe old man soon died. When the son heard of his father&amp;rsquo;s death, he felt dejected and when he was told of the contents of the will, he felt worse. The villagers gathered to share out the property in accordance with what was stated in the will. The boy was confused as to which part of the property to choose. His father&amp;rsquo;s closest friend, who had come to console him, advised him. The boy cleared his throat and&lt;br /&gt;\nannounced that what he wanted was the chief slave himself since, after all, he was a part his father&amp;rsquo;s property.&lt;br /&gt;\nIn this way, the son inherited his father&amp;rsquo;s entire property because whatever belonged to the chief slave belonged to the master of the chief slave. The villagers were happy at the young boy&amp;rsquo;s wise choice but the chief slave was extremely upset by it.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;The old man stated in his &amp;lsquo;will&amp;rsquo; that his son should.&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;have the chief slave&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;have only one item from his property&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;kill the chief slave&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;be given all his property&lt;/p&gt;\n','set all the slaves free','B',1,'',64),(67,'&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;INSTRUCTIONS: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Answer all questions in this section.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;em&gt;Read the two passages below carefully and answer the questions under them. Shade the&lt;br /&gt;\nanswer space in the answer sheet which bears the same letter as the answer you have&lt;br /&gt;\nchosen. &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;PASSAGE ONE &lt;/strong&gt;Once upon a time, in the village of Jassaca, there lived a rich old man. The man had only one son but many slaves. He thought about what was best to do for the boy and decided to send him to school to acquire knowledge which would help him to spend his wealth sensibly.&lt;br /&gt;\nWhile the young boy was away, the old man became seriously ill and fearing that he might die, he decided to write his will. He was worried about how many of his property was best to leave to his son and what to do with the slaves. Fearing that the slaves might kill his son if he left all his property to him, he decided to leave his entire property to the chief slave except for one small part of it which he would allow his son to choose. The chief slave was happy at this news, since it meant that he would inherit&lt;br /&gt;\nalmost the entire property and the son would have only a part of it.&lt;br /&gt;\nThe old man soon died. When the son heard of his father&amp;rsquo;s death, he felt dejected and when he was told of the contents of the will, he felt worse. The villagers gathered to share out the property in accordance with what was stated in the will. The boy was confused as to which part of the property to choose. His father&amp;rsquo;s closest friend, who had come to console him, advised him. The boy cleared his throat and&lt;br /&gt;\nannounced that what he wanted was the chief slave himself since, after all, he was a part his father&amp;rsquo;s property.&lt;br /&gt;\nIn this way, the son inherited his father&amp;rsquo;s entire property because whatever belonged to the chief slave belonged to the master of the chief slave. The villagers were happy at the young boy&amp;rsquo;s wise choice but the chief slave was extremely upset by it.&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;The chief slave was upset when the son chose him because&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;he did not like the son&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;the son was wicked&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;it meant that he could not have any of the property&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;the villagers were happy&lt;/p&gt;\n','the son’s friends consoled him','C',1,'',65),(68,'&lt;p style=&quot;margin-left:0cm; margin-right:0cm&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:11pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;PASSAGE TWO&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;p style=&quot;margin-left:0cm; margin-right:0cm&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:11pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;No cloth can be made without thread. Thread is made from a number of different things. A lot of thread,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;for example, is made from wool. The wool of animals like the sheep, goat and even the camel is used for&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;making cloth. Of these, the commonest is the wool of the sheep.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;When the wool is cut from a sheep, it is not yet ready to be made into cloth. First, it must be washed and&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;cleaned until the dirt has been taken away and only the soft, clean wool is left. This is then spun into&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;thread. Cloth made from wool has great warmth and is mostly worn in cold countries.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;Clothes are also made of cotton. Cotton comes from a plant. When they are ready for harvest, the white&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;balls of cotton hair are picked from the plant and sent to the factory where they are spun into cotton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;thread. Then they are taken to a weaving factory to be made into cloth. Because people in hot countries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;want to wear something which is light and cool, most of their clothes are made of cotton.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;There are many other kinds of cloth. Silk, for example, is made from silk hair spun by tiny caterpillars.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;p style=&quot;margin-left:0cm; margin-right:0cm&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:11pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;Making real silk is slow and difficult. Because of this, real silk cost a lot of money. Most people would&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;rather buy artificial silk or one of the other artificial clothes, like nylon which is so common nowadays.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;<br /><hr />Cloth is made from different natural materials except','cotton','wool from animals','silk','leaves','leaves','D',1,'',66),(69,'&lt;p style=&quot;margin-left:0cm; margin-right:0cm&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:11pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;PASSAGE TWO&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;p style=&quot;margin-left:0cm; margin-right:0cm&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:11pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;No cloth can be made without thread. Thread is made from a number of different things. A lot of thread,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;for example, is made from wool. The wool of animals like the sheep, goat and even the camel is used for&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;making cloth. Of these, the commonest is the wool of the sheep.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;When the wool is cut from a sheep, it is not yet ready to be made into cloth. First, it must be washed and&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;cleaned until the dirt has been taken away and only the soft, clean wool is left. This is then spun into&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;thread. Cloth made from wool has great warmth and is mostly worn in cold countries.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;Clothes are also made of cotton. Cotton comes from a plant. When they are ready for harvest, the white&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;balls of cotton hair are picked from the plant and sent to the factory where they are spun into cotton&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;thread. Then they are taken to a weaving factory to be made into cloth. Because people in hot countries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;want to wear something which is light and cool, most of their clothes are made of cotton.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;\n&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;There are many other kinds of cloth. Silk, for example, is made from silk hair spun by tiny caterpillars.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;p style=&quot;margin-left:0cm; margin-right:0cm&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:11pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;Making real silk is slow and difficult. Because of this, real silk cost a lot of money. Most people would&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Cambria,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;rather buy artificial silk or one of the other artificial clothes, like nylon which is so common nowadays.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;hr /&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Wool cut from a sheep is not immediately ready to be made into cloth because it is:&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;hard&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;soft&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;dirty&lt;/p&gt;\n','&lt;p&gt;not white&lt;/p&gt;\n','artificial','C',1,'',67),(70,'<strong>PASSAGE TWO</strong><br />No cloth can be made without thread. Thread is made from a number of different things. A lot of thread, for example, is made from wool. The wool of animals like the sheep, goat and even the camel is used for making cloth. Of these, the commonest is the wool of the sheep. <br/>\nWhen the wool is cut from a sheep, it is not yet ready to be made into cloth. First, it must be washed and cleaned until the dirt has been taken away and only the soft, clean wool is left. This is then spun into thread. Cloth made from wool has great warmth and is mostly worn in cold countries. <br/>\nClothes are also made of cotton. Cotton comes from a plant. When they are ready for harvest, the white balls of cotton hair are picked from the plant and sent to the factory where they are spun into cotton thread. Then they are taken to a weaving factory to be made into cloth. Because people in hot countries want to wear something which is light and cool, most of their clothes are made of cotton.\nThere are many other kinds of cloth. Silk, for example, is made from silk hair spun by tiny caterpillars. <br/>\nMaking real silk is slow and difficult. Because of this, real silk cost a lot of money. Most people would rather buy artificial silk or one of the other artificial clothes, like nylon which is so common nowadays.\n<br /><hr />Why is woolen cloth mostly used in cold countries?','Sheep like cold weather','Wool has great warmth','Wool cloths are heavy','Wool comes from animals','Wool is cheap','B',1,'',68),(71,'&lt;p style=&quot;margin-left:0cm; margin-right:0cm&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:11pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:Calibri,sans-serif&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-family:&amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;,serif&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color:black&quot;&gt;PASSAGE TWO&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;\n\n&lt;p style=&quot;margin-left:0cm; margin-right:0cm&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;fo